GitOps Repository Structure 

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring a very important topic for real-world DevOps projects: GitOps Repository Structure. 🚀
A well-organized repository structure helps teams manage applications, configurations, and environments efficiently.

🔹 App Repository
 The application repository contains the actual application code.
 Typical contents include:
Source code
Dockerfile
Build configurations
CI pipeline definitions
This repository is mainly used by developers to build and update the application.

🔹 Config Repository
 The configuration repository stores infrastructure and deployment configurations.
Examples include:
Kubernetes YAML manifests
Deployment configurations
Service definitions
GitOps tools monitor this repository and use it to deploy applications into the cluster.

🔹 Environment Folders (Dev / Stage / Prod)
 In real-world projects, deployments are separated by environments such as:
Dev → for development and testing
Stage → for pre-production validation
Prod → for live production systems
Each environment has its own configuration files to ensure controlled and safe deployments.

Example structure:
gitops-config/
 ├── dev/
 │ └── deployment.yaml
 ├── stage/
 │ └── deployment.yaml
 └── prod/
 └── deployment.yaml

This structure allows teams to manage multiple environments while keeping everything version-controlled in Git.

💡 Key Insight:
 A clear GitOps repository structure improves deployment consistency, collaboration, and environment management.
